WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:07.810
This is Ipley Cross, in the middle of the
New Forest, in south-west England.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.400
I couldn't find any official statistics for
the most dangerous crossroads in Britain,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.690
but this is certainly a contender.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.680
Because even though it's quiet and rural here,
there's not much traffic,

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.060
there's an average of
two incidents a year here.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.860
Two cyclists have died in the last ten years
in near-identical crashes:

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.580
both times, a driver failed to stop

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.279
and hit a cyclist who had
right-of-way on the main road.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:32.330
But the visibility is excellent here.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.360
It's well-signed.
Actually, really, really well signed,

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.980
now I'm here it's obvious there's
a lot of road markings for such a rural junction.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.840
But people keep getting hurt here.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:43.540
So why is it so bad?

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.500
For the research here, I'm indebted to
someone called Bez.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.880
That's the only name I could find, 
I couldn't track them down,

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.600
they didn't reply to messages,

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:55.850
but Bez wrote the definitive article
on why this place is such a disaster.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:59.190
Let's say there's a driver approaching on
the side road at around 40mph.

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.640
They're not slowing down,
because they're reckless,

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.740
and they can see the main road is clear.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:04.700
Here's their view.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:10.220
The cyclist, until the very last second, was
exactly in the blind spot

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:12.630
caused by the driver's door pillar.

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.640
The driver didn't slow down for the stop sign,
which would have shown them the cyclist.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:18.591
They didn't move their head, which would have
shown them the cyclist.

00:01:18.591 --> 00:01:21.460
The speed involved doesn't have to be some
magic bad number.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:23.520
Because of the angle of these roads,

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.960
for every possible speed that
a reckless driver might be travelling at,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.240
there is a reasonable speed for
a cyclist on the main road

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:34.960
that'll keep them right in that blind spot
all the way.

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:37.249
But why won't the cyclist see the car?

00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:41.640
They have right of way, yes, but surely they'd
notice that the car's not slowing down.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:44.820
Well, if these two roads were crossing at
right angles to each other,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.240
yes, they would see them.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:48.940
Don't get me wrong, the driver would still
be reckless,

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:52.859
but at least the cyclist would notice
and be able to take evasive action.

00:01:52.859 --> 00:01:55.460
But these roads aren't at right angles.

00:01:55.460 --> 00:02:02.170
There's one other subtle thing here: in this situation,
the car is approaching from behind the cyclist.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:04.569
The cyclist won't see it until it's too late.

00:02:04.569 --> 00:02:08.580
And even if they do, when both car and bicycle
slam on their brakes,

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.940
simple simulations don't work any more .

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.100
Because it's not up to trigonometry
at that point, it's up to human reactions

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.580
and how both of them try to avoid the collision.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.810
This junction is at the worst possible angle.

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.400
And it happened by accident:
you can see on old maps

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.520
that this was just the angle at which
two paths happened to intersect,

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.980
and those were the paths that
happened to become roads over time.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.260
This wouldn't be built nowadays: but at some
point in the early 20th century,

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.220
before it was possible to travel by road
at these sorts of speeds,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.170
this was a reasonable junction.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.360
The local council has made some changes. Not only the paint:

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.680
but you can see on Google Street View that
the signs here used to be 'give way', not 'stop'.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.180
Stop signs are really rare in Britain,

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:53.540
they're only meant to be used in
exceptional circumstances like this,

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:56.020
and almost everyone just
rolls through them anyway.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:01.120
And they're clearly not working, because people
keep getting hurt.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:02.940
So why hasn't it been redesigned?

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:05.100
Speed bumps or a chicane on the approach would
solve the problem,

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.660
but it's not legal to have those
unless you also have street lighting,

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.930
which would mean laying miles of
new electrical cable at enormous expense.

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.540
That's assuming you could get around
all the regulations

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:17.020
that define this area
as a conservation area,

00:03:17.020 --> 00:03:20.280
and the objections from the locals
about putting bright lighting

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:23.260
in a place famed for its dark skies.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:25.730
Same problem with a traffic light.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:29.940
The best solution is to stagger the crossing,
to curve the two side roads

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:35.300
so they meet the main road at right angles,
force the drivers to slow down.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.300
But again: conservation area,
conservative locals,

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.510
and a budget estimated at around £100,000.

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.300
The cheapest and easiest way to solve it,
of course,

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:46.760
would be for drivers to stop at
the stop sign.

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:51.940
But road safety, and anyone's safety, 
shouldn't require faith in human nature.

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.820
Thank you to Bez, whoever you are!

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.800
The research for this video
started with that article, so

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.160
I've put a link in the description,
please do go and read it.

